- backboard: rectangle from rim x+ .15unit to +? Standard: rim extends from backboard toward court (left). Backboard at pole x-? From side view: pole at x=.98 world, backboard at x=1.02? Rim at x=.92. So backboard vertical rectangle at x = rimX + unit*.22, width unit*.06 (edge-on), height unit*.55, y centered above rim. But edge-on looks like thin line. Draw semi-transparent white glass rectangle with a border and orange target square — reads as backboard from 3/4. Let's draw backboard as a vertical rectangle centered at rimX+unit*.28, width unit*.5?? Hmm if front-facing, width ~1.8m vs height 1.05m; but on side view we wouldn't see width. Stylized 2D games show it front-facing, which is more recognizable. I'll draw backboard face-on: rect width unit*0.62, height unit*0.42, centered at rimX + unit*0.34, bottom aligned slightly above rim. Rim extends left from bottom center. Pole behind at right. This is a typical 2D depiction and looks fine.

Actually with players to the left shooting right, hoop/backboard on right edge. Rim at x rimX, backboard face-on at x rimX+0.2*unit (center), width maybe 55px; extends left over rim? Rim attaches at board left edge; rim center ~ boardLeft - rimRadius. Let's define:
